    blindfoldURL = "In the late 1970s, Aishe commands a crowded occult booth inside a disco-era nightlife venue, turning commercialized mystique into genuine influence. She flips a card toward a predatory promoter while simultaneously warning a queer couple away from a police vice raid forming outside. Mirror-ball reflections, cassette equipment, neon, tourist occult fashion, and deep-red highlights surround her, but Aishe remains the sharp, active center rather than a glamorous pose.",
    splashText = "COMMON INSTRUCTIONS\nCreate one textless 1920x1080 concept-sampler image for Aishe-26, divided into four equal 16:9 quads in a clean 2x2 grid: Quad A top-left, Quad B top-right, Quad C bottom-left, and Quad D bottom-right. Each quad must occupy exactly one quarter of the canvas and depict a different unified frozen moment suitable for later expansion into a full 1920x1080 Memoriam splash. Use a narrow neutral-black cross-shaped gutter; do not let figures, architecture, smoke, weather, or lighting cross between quads.\n\nREFERENCE INPUTS — Attach these files as actual image inputs; do not merely mention them in text. Attach `assets/input/character-intro-splashes/combined/memoriam-splash-style-contact-sheet.png` and use it only as a rendering and composition guide: cinematic gothic-noir realism, near-monochrome black, charcoal, and cold silver, hard contrast, deep negative space, wet or metallic highlights, layered atmospheric depth, and restrained blood-red accents. Do not copy its characters, Toronto locations, symbols, readable words, or exact compositions. Attach `assets/input/character-intro-splashes/characters/aishe.webp` as the exact identity reference for Aishe Tache; preserve her facial structure, build, apparent adult age, and commanding beauty while replacing modern clothing and background with historically appropriate content. Attach `assets/input/character-intro-splashes/npcs/Prince Marcel Guilbeau.webp` as exact identity reference for Marcel Guilbeau; preserve that character’s face, apparent age, build, and distinguishing features while adapting clothing, grooming, and accessories to the depicted year.\n\nCHARACTER AND PERIOD — Set all four concepts within 1955–1983, New Orleans, United States. Aishe is an ageless vampire after her Embrace: preserve the same apparent age and identity across all four quads even when the depicted decade changes. Adapt every costume, hairstyle, furnishing, vehicle, weapon, street, and social custom to the specific decade shown in its quad. Aishe is poised, incisive, socially commanding, and difficult to read; show her actively making choices, reading people, negotiating, protecting, manipulating, or surviving rather than passively posing. Her Tarot and visions may be suggested through cards, reflections, prophetic shadows, or restrained double exposure, never generic magical beams or colorful fantasy spell effects.\n\nAISHE’S PHYSIQUE — Treat Aishe’s body shape as an identity invariant in every quad. She is tall, broad-framed, big-boned, and visibly full-figured, with substantial shoulders, arms, hips, thighs, and a soft but powerful build. Do not slim, narrow, or delicately proportion her, and do not depict a pregnancy silhouette. Keep her hands actively occupied, gesturing, braced on furniture, holding an object, or relaxed at her sides—never resting on, cradling, or protectively framing her stomach.\n\nCAMERA AND STYLE — Keep the chronicle’s primary high-contrast black-and-white gothic-noir language with selective deep-red accents. Across the sampler, use mostly clear cinematic framing, but give one or two quads a purposeful low angle, high angle, restrained Dutch tilt, over-the-shoulder viewpoint, or foreground obstruction where it strengthens power, vulnerability, surveillance, or disorientation. Avoid fisheye distortion, broken anatomy, pin-up posing, romanticized intimacy, or static character lineups. Keep named figures visually separated enough to read as active participants. Reserve a calm, dark, low-detail area inside every quad for later time, location, and narrative overlays. No montage within a quad, no captions, labels, logos, borders beyond the central gutter, watermarks, modern anachronisms, or graphic gore.\n\nQUAD A — TOP LEFT\nAt the first restored court ceremony after Doran’s death, Marcel Guilbeau receives petitions beneath polished Creole chandeliers while Aishe stands to one side with an unreadable Tarot card. Marcel gestures for her endorsement; instead, she redirects a frightened mortal petitioner away from his retainers and toward the door. The active refusal should show Aishe preserving Status while emotionally withdrawing from a hollow regime.\n\nQUAD B — TOP RIGHT\nIn Aishe’s small neighborhood fortune shop during the early 1960s, she leans across a worn table and firmly returns a wedding ring to an unnamed middle-class client who has asked whether to remain in an unhappy marriage. Other women wait among secondhand furniture, handwritten appointment slips without readable text, and streetcar light. Aishe’s expression is compassionate but unsentimental; the moment should show truthful counsel restoring her humanity.\n\nQUAD C — BOTTOM LEFT\nDuring a civil-rights demonstration in the late 1960s, Aishe pulls two shop clients through a side doorway as police charge along the New Orleans street outside. She uses a glimpse in a shop mirror to anticipate the danger, shoves the bolt closed, and points an injured organizer toward a rear passage. Protest signs remain unreadable; rain, siren light, and one restrained red scarf provide accents. Frame through the shattered front window for urgency and layered depth.\n\nQUAD D — BOTTOM RIGHT\nIn the late 1970s, Aishe commands a crowded occult booth inside a disco-era nightlife venue, turning commercialized mystique into genuine influence. She flips a card toward a predatory promoter while simultaneously warning a queer couple away from a police vice raid forming outside. Mirror-ball reflections, cassette equipment, neon, tourist occult fashion, and deep-red highlights surround her, but Aishe remains the sharp, active center rather than a glamorous pose.",
