    blindfoldURL = "Inside a cramped captain’s cabin late in the period, Caesar turns a planned punishment into mutiny by slamming a navigation chart across the table and pointing armed deckhands toward their targets. A pistol skids across the floor, the ship lists under cannon fire, and a Spanish Florida coastline lies beyond the stern windows. The moment shows the feared “Black Caesar” legend emerging from resistance and maritime competence without depicting a named historical pirate.",
    splashText = "COMMON INSTRUCTIONS\nCreate one textless 1920x1080 concept-sampler image for BlackCaesar-6, divided into four equal 16:9 quads in a clean 2x2 grid: Quad A top-left, Quad B top-right, Quad C bottom-left, and Quad D bottom-right. Each quad must occupy exactly one quarter of the canvas and depict a different unified frozen moment suitable for later expansion into a full 1920x1080 Memoriam splash. Use a narrow neutral-black cross-shaped gutter; do not let figures, architecture, smoke, weather, ships, or lighting cross between quads.\n\nREFERENCE INPUTS — Attach these files as actual image inputs. Attach `assets/input/character-intro-splashes/combined/memoriam-splash-style-contact-sheet.png` only as the rendering and composition guide: cinematic gothic-noir realism, near-monochrome black, charcoal, and cold silver, hard contrast, deep negative space, wet or metallic highlights, layered atmospheric depth, and restrained blood-red accents. Do not copy its characters, Toronto locations, symbols, readable words, or exact compositions. Attach `assets/input/character-intro-splashes/characters/black-caesar.webp` as the exact identity reference for Black Caesar; preserve his facial structure, dark skin, compact muscular build, apparent adult age, and commanding presence while replacing modern clothing and background with historically appropriate content. Black Caesar is short for an adult man and is often the shortest grown person in the room. Keep him visibly muscular and physically capable, but never tall, oversized, towering, broad-framed, or larger than the adults around him; his authority comes from focus, skill, and presence rather than stature.\n\nCHARACTER AND PERIOD — Set all concepts within 1720–1734, the Caribbean. Caesar is mortal throughout this period; show natural aging while preserving the identity of the adult reference. Adapt ships, weapons, architecture, uniforms, clothing, furnishings, documents, and transport to the specific decade and geography of each quad. Caesar is a disciplined survivor, mariner, occult operative, and later commander: depict him actively navigating, bargaining, fighting, directing, studying, or choosing—not passively posing. Treat slavery, colonial violence, famine, war, and racial oppression with gravity rather than adventure-romance. Blood Sorcery may appear as restrained geometric skeins or seals of deep-red light. Ghosts, wraiths, and Lwa should appear as cold ash-grey translucence, shadow, breath-like mist, or possession expressed through a host’s body; do not use Chimerstry’s purple smoke language or generic fantasy beams.\n\nCAMERA AND STYLE — Keep the chronicle’s primary high-contrast black-and-white gothic-noir language with selective deep-red accents. Use mostly clear cinematic framing, with one or two purposeful low angles, high angles, restrained Dutch tilts, over-the-shoulder viewpoints, or foreground obstructions where they strengthen danger, command, confinement, or surveillance. Avoid fisheye distortion, broken anatomy, static lineups, triumphalist colonial imagery, pirate caricature, or glossy superhero posing. Keep named characters sufficiently separated for readable interaction. Reserve a calm, dark, low-detail area inside every quad for later overlays. No montage within a quad, captions, labels, readable documents, logos, extra borders, watermarks, modern anachronisms, or graphic gore.\n\nQUAD A — TOP LEFT\nOn the deck of a weather-beaten merchant vessel at night, enslaved Caesar works the helm through a sudden squall while an armed overseer orders exhausted sailors below. Caesar secretly alters the heading toward shoals and studies the keys at the man’s belt. Wet rigging, torn canvas, cargo chains, and distant lightning convey coerced maritime expertise and the first calculation of escape, not romantic piracy.\n\nQUAD B — TOP RIGHT\nAt a crowded Nassau waterfront sale, Caesar stands chained among sailors and laborers as competing captains inspect them beside captured cargo. Use an over-the-shoulder view from behind a ledger and raised bidding hand, keeping Caesar’s controlled stare in sharp focus. Taverns, repair yards, fort guns, and anchored ships establish a violent economy of ownership; Caesar notices an unattended knife and a narrow route to the water.\n\nQUAD C — BOTTOM LEFT\nIn a mangrove channel under moonlight, Caesar guides a stolen skiff through roots while armed pursuers search the beach with lanterns. He silences frightened fellow fugitives with one hand and uses the other to pole them toward open water. The scene should emphasize navigation, solidarity under pressure, and the constant danger of recapture, with a low waterline camera making the mangroves feel enclosing.\n\nQUAD D — BOTTOM RIGHT\nInside a cramped captain’s cabin late in the period, Caesar turns a planned punishment into mutiny by slamming a navigation chart across the table and pointing armed deckhands toward their targets. A pistol skids across the floor, the ship lists under cannon fire, and a Spanish Florida coastline lies beyond the stern windows. The moment shows the feared “Black Caesar” legend emerging from resistance and maritime competence without depicting a named historical pirate.",
    npcs = {
      {
        label = "",
        tokenURL = "FALSE",
