    blindfoldURL = "Inside a lamplit Port-au-Prince ritual room near the end of the period, Black Caesar is extending a sealed paper envelope across a long table directly toward Jervais de la Croix. Caesar is shorter than Jervais and is looking up into his sire's eyes with a firm, unsmiling expression. Jervais is reaching toward the envelope with one gloved hand while staring back at Caesar, his brows raised in offended disbelief. The Baron's Bleak Bokor is standing at Caesar's side in an early nineteenth-century Haitian dark frock coat, patterned waistcoat, high-collared linen shirt, ritual beads, and small protective charms. The Bokor is wearing no eyewear; his bare mysterious brown eyes and the makeup continuing around them are clearly visible as he is looking at Jervais with chin lifted. No other person is standing between the three named figures. Distant ship lanterns are visible through a black window. The camera is beside the envelope at table height, making the handoff and three eyelines unmistakable.",
    splashText = "COMMON INSTRUCTIONS\nCreate one textless 1920x1080 concept-sampler image for BlackCaesar-18, divided into four equal 16:9 quads in a clean 2x2 grid: Quad A top-left, Quad B top-right, Quad C bottom-left, and Quad D bottom-right. Every quad is occupying exactly one quarter of the canvas. A narrow neutral-black cross-shaped gutter is separating the quads, with no visual content crossing the gutter.\n\nREFERENCE INPUTS — Attach every named file as an actual generation input. Attach `assets/input/character-intro-splashes/combined/memoriam-splash-style-contact-sheet.png` only as a style reference for cinematic gothic-noir realism, near-monochrome black, charcoal, and cold silver, hard contrast, deep negative space, wet or metallic highlights, layered atmospheric depth, and restrained deep-red accents. Its people, locations, words, symbols, and exact compositions are not source content. Attach `assets/input/character-intro-splashes/characters/black-caesar.webp` as the exact identity reference for Black Caesar. He is retaining the same facial structure, dark skin, dense curly black hair, compact muscular build, and apparent adult age in every quad. His upper lip, chin, and jaw are completely clean-shaven. He is a short adult man; beside standing adult men, the top of his head and shoulders are visibly lower than theirs. His authority is being communicated through posture, skill, attention, and others' reactions rather than height or bulk. Attach `assets/input/character-intro-splashes/npcs/The Baron's Bleak Bokor.webp` as the identity reference for The Baron's Bleak Bokor; preserve the referenced facial structure, complexion, distinguishing features, and recognizable identity while adapting apparent age, supernatural state, clothing, and grooming only as explicitly required by a quad. His eyes are bare and fully visible, with facial makeup continuing around his mysterious brown eyes; he is wearing no glasses or sunglasses. Attach `assets/input/character-intro-splashes/npcs/Jervais de la Croix.webp` as the identity reference for Jervais de la Croix; preserve the referenced facial structure, complexion, distinguishing features, and recognizable identity while adapting apparent age, supernatural state, clothing, and grooming only as explicitly required by a quad. Attach `assets/input/character-intro-splashes/npcs/Pre-Death Jean Ducheski I.webp` as the exact identity reference for Jean Ducheski I. Jean is a Black man with deep brown skin. Preserve the reference's recognizable facial structure, complexion, dark eyes, and distinguishing features at every age. Quad A is aging him down to a lean fifteen-year-old Black teenage boy in 1800 while retaining the same deep-brown complexion and recognizable younger version of the referenced face. Quad C is showing him as a young Black adult man before his 1830 death, again retaining the reference's facial identity and complexion. His clothing and grooming are being adapted only for the exact decade. Do not lighten his skin or depict him as white.\n\nFROZEN-IMAGE RULE — Each quad is depicting one decisive instant in one location, not a synopsis, montage, or sequence. Every featured person is having one principal pose or physical action, one explicit eyeline, and one visibly described expression. Narrative context is being communicated only through bodies, spatial relationships, props, architecture, lighting, and background evidence. Each quad is remaining intelligible without knowledge of Vampire: the Masquerade terminology.\n\nPERIOD — All concepts are taking place within 1796–1846, Port-au-Prince, Haiti. Caesar is an ageless vampire throughout this period. Exterior scenes are taking place at night, and interiors have lamplight or candlelight with dark windows. Jean Ducheski I is a fifteen-year-old newly appointed first mate in 1800, is aging as a mortal thereafter, and is dead after 1830. Ships, weapons, architecture, uniforms, clothing, furnishings, documents, and transport are being rendered accurately for the relevant decade and geography. Colonial violence, slavery, racial oppression, warfare, and religious practice are being treated with gravity rather than adventure-romance or exotic spectacle.\n\nSUPERNATURAL VISUAL LANGUAGE — Blood Sorcery is appearing as a small number of clean, glowing red-white lines of energy, geometric seals, or luminous contours rather than webbing. Voodoo ritual geometry is appearing as recognizable veves: flowing symmetrical linework with crossroads, curves, and ritual flourishes rather than alchemical circles or generic pentagrams. Wraiths are appearing as cold ash-grey translucent human forms with softened lower bodies, drifting edges, and the recognizable faces and period clothing of the dead.\n\nCAMERA AND STYLE — The alternatives are exploring meaningfully different framing: eye-level interaction, low foreground angle, high or overhead angle, over-the-shoulder view, restrained Dutch tilt, or foreground obstruction where useful. Every angle is preserving readable anatomy, expressions, eyelines, stature, and focal interaction. Each quad is reserving one calm, dark, low-detail region for later overlay text. The complete sampler contains no captions, labels, readable paragraphs, logos, extra borders, watermarks, modern objects, graphic gore, fisheye distortion, static lineup, or glossy superhero posing.\n\nQUAD A — TOP LEFT\nAt a damaged Port-au-Prince harbor around 1800 beneath a black smoke-streaked night sky, Black Caesar is standing at the near corner of a rough map table while pressing one finger onto a marked harbor entrance. He is looking up toward a local commander opposite him; his brows are knitted and his mouth is slightly open in urgent explanation. Fifteen-year-old Jean Ducheski I is standing directly beside Caesar as his newly appointed first mate. Jean is unmistakably a Black teenage boy with deep brown skin, the same dark eyes and facial structure as the attached `Pre-Death Jean Ducheski I.webp` identity reference age-adjusted to fifteen, a lean adolescent build, and practical sailor's clothing rather than an officer's uniform. Jean is only slightly taller than short adult Caesar. He is following Caesar's pointing finger with an intent, serious expression and both hands resting at his sides. Local commanders, sailors, healers, and ritual leaders are occupying the remaining sides of the table. Artillery flashes are glowing distantly beyond market roofs. The camera is low along the tabletop, with the map, Caesar, and young Jean forming the foreground.\n\nQUAD B — TOP RIGHT\nInside a candlelit cemetery courtyard, The Baron's Bleak Bokor is gripping the wrist of a seated possessed participant while Black Caesar is holding one open palm above a flowing Voodoo veve drawn on the stone between them. Caesar is visibly shorter than the Bokor and is looking at the participant's displaced ash-grey shadow with narrowed, attentive eyes. The Bokor's uncovered brown eyes are fixed on the participant's face; his brows are calm and his painted mouth is set in solemn concentration. The participant's body is rigid while the human-shaped shadow behind the chair is leaning toward Caesar independently. Houngans, mambos, revenants, and grieving relatives are forming a respectful semicircle at a distance. The camera is over the participant's shoulder, making Caesar, the Bokor, the veve, and the impossible shadow one readable arrangement.\n\nQUAD C — BOTTOM LEFT\nIn the courtyard of a mature House High Saturday compound during the 1820s, under a black night sky, Black Caesar is holding a ceremonial vessel above a trade ledger lying on a central table. He is looking from the raised vessel toward two disputing representatives across the table, his brows lowered and his lips pressed into an authoritative line. Caesar is visibly shorter than the standing representatives. Adult Jean Ducheski I is standing taller at Caesar's right as his mortal first mate. Jean is unmistakably a young Black adult man with deep brown skin, the same dark eyes and facial structure as the attached `Pre-Death Jean Ducheski I.webp` identity reference, and period-accurate sailor's clothing. He is watching the disputants with his arms folded and a severe expression. Merchants, ritual specialists, Ducheski revenants, and foreign visitors are occupying separate benches behind the two parties. Harbor warehouses and a military square are visible through the open gate in darkness. The camera is high above the table, using the vessel and ledger as the visual division between the groups.\n\nQUAD D — BOTTOM RIGHT\nInside a lamplit Port-au-Prince ritual room near the end of the period, Black Caesar is extending a sealed paper envelope across a long table directly toward Jervais de la Croix. Caesar is shorter than Jervais and is looking up into his sire's eyes with a firm, unsmiling expression. Jervais is reaching toward the envelope with one gloved hand while staring back at Caesar, his brows raised in offended disbelief. The Baron's Bleak Bokor is standing at Caesar's side in an early nineteenth-century Haitian dark frock coat, patterned waistcoat, high-collared linen shirt, ritual beads, and small protective charms. The Bokor is wearing no eyewear; his bare mysterious brown eyes and the makeup continuing around them are clearly visible as he is looking at Jervais with chin lifted. No other person is standing between the three named figures. Distant ship lanterns are visible through a black window. The camera is beside the envelope at table height, making the handoff and three eyelines unmistakable.",
