    blindfoldURL = "Use a first-person point of view through Drake’s eyes immediately after the Embrace, looking steeply down at Fomórach lying beneath him in fresh snow beside a ruined frontier shelter. Drake himself must never appear as a visible person: show no face, head, silhouette, or exposed body; at most, the extreme lower edge may contain one indistinct dark-wrapped forearm matching his reference. Fomórach lies on his back in torn late-eighteenth-century frontier clothing, staring upward in shock and reaching weakly toward the viewer. Two small, clean fang marks are visible at the side of his neck with no blood, open wound, or gore. From those marks, branching black veins spread beneath his pallid skin like ink corruption or frost cracks, visibly beginning the Wendigo curse and the transition from his recognizable human identity toward his later Nosferatu form. Snow falls through moonlight around him and gathers on his hair, shoulders, broken timbers, and dead leaves; his breath has stopped while the surrounding forest remains violently alive. Keep the transformation localized and readable rather than grotesque, and reserve a broad calm, dark, low-detail area for overlay text.",
    splashText = "COMMON INSTRUCTIONS\nCreate one textless 1920x1080 concept-sampler image for Fomórach-5, divided into four equal 16:9 quads in a clean 2x2 grid: Quad A top-left, Quad B top-right, Quad C bottom-left, and Quad D bottom-right. Each quad must occupy exactly one quarter of the canvas and depict a different unified frozen moment suitable for later expansion into a full 1920x1080 Memoriam splash. Use a narrow neutral-black cross-shaped gutter; do not let figures, architecture, smoke, weather, or lighting cross between quads.\n\nREFERENCE INPUTS — Attach these files as actual image inputs. Attach `assets/input/character-intro-splashes/combined/memoriam-splash-style-contact-sheet.png` only as the rendering and composition guide: cinematic gothic-noir realism, near-monochrome black, charcoal, and cold silver, hard contrast, deep negative space, wet or metallic highlights, layered atmospheric depth, and restrained blood-red accents. Do not copy its characters, Toronto locations, symbols, readable words, or exact compositions. Attach `assets/input/character-intro-splashes/npcs/Pre-Embrace Fomorach.webp` as Fomórach’s exact pre-Embrace identity reference, and attach `assets/input/character-intro-splashes/characters/fomorach.webp` as his exact post-Embrace identity reference; follow the mortal/Embrace instruction below for which version appears in each quad. Attach `assets/input/character-intro-splashes/npcs/Drake.webp` as exact identity reference for Drake; preserve each named figure’s face, apparent age, build, and distinguishing features while adapting costume and grooming to the period.\n\nCHARACTER AND PERIOD — Set all concepts within 1786–1793, the failing East Coast colonies. The period crosses ghouldom and Embrace: keep Fomórach recognizably the same young man while making the transition from mortal hardship to the visibly inhuman curse of his Nosferatu blood unmistakable. Adapt architecture, clothing, tools, transport, weapons, and infrastructure to the exact decade and geography of each quad. Fomórach is an overlooked listener, survivor, ferocious protector, and infrastructure-minded intelligence gatherer. Show him actively eavesdropping, directing, tunneling, rescuing, hunting, confronting, or making a disastrous choice—not passively posing. Obfuscate and supernatural stealth should read through swallowed light, impossible shadow, or figures overlooking him rather than colorful invisibility effects. Hunger may appear through gaunt tension, frost-like breath, sharpened fangs, and restrained blood-red eye or mouth accents, never glamourized feeding.\n\nQUAD-SPECIFIC IDENTITY LOCK — Quads A, B, and C depict only pre-Embrace Fomórach using the human reference: an ordinary living human face and anatomy, with no Nosferatu deformities, corpse pallor, fangs, glowing or predatory red eyes, or other vampiric traits. Ghouldom in Quads B and C affects only his endurance, deprivation, and craving; it does not alter his human appearance. Only Quad D may begin the transition toward the post-Embrace Nosferatu reference and visible deformities.\n\nCAMERA AND STYLE — Keep the chronicle’s primary high-contrast black-and-white gothic-noir language with selective deep-red accents. Use mostly clear cinematic framing, with one or two purposeful low angles, high angles, restrained Dutch tilts, listening-wall viewpoints, or foreground obstructions where they strengthen secrecy, confinement, hunger, or sudden violence. Avoid fisheye distortion, broken anatomy, static lineups, monster pin-up posing, or modern horror clichés. Treat famine, colonial violence, war, homelessness, and displacement with gravity. Reserve a calm, dark, low-detail area inside every quad for later overlays. No montage within a quad, captions, labels, readable documents, logos, extra borders, watermarks, modern anachronisms, or graphic gore.\n\nQUAD A — TOP LEFT\nNewly arrived under indenture, mortal Fomórach stands in a communal store and demonstrates that the colony’s ration account cannot match its supplies while Puritan officials dismiss him as an uneducated Catholic servant. He points laborers toward a concealed leak in the roof and quietly pockets evidence of theft. Fresh cabins, unfinished fields, muddy tools, and dense forest show a settlement already failing.\n\nQUAD B — TOP RIGHT\nFrom a listening-wall viewpoint inside a rough tavern, ghouled Fomórach presses close to split timber while officials, hunters, and preachers discuss disappearances on the other side. Drake waits unseen beneath the floor hatch as Fomórach passes him a coded object through the gap. The scene should show his 'resting dumb face' as camouflage, his intelligence value, and the secrecy of their early relationship.\n\nQUAD C — BOTTOM LEFT\nIn a snowbound winter storehouse, starving ghouled Fomórach distributes the last food to delirious settlers while resisting a covered vessel of vampire blood that Drake has left for him. His body is gaunt and trembling but his attention remains fixed on overheard accusations outside. Frost, abandoned tools, failing watchfires, and empty shelves make supernatural deprivation indistinguishable from colonial scarcity.\n\nQUAD D — BOTTOM RIGHT\nUse a first-person point of view through Drake’s eyes immediately after the Embrace, looking steeply down at Fomórach lying beneath him in fresh snow beside a ruined frontier shelter. Drake himself must never appear as a visible person: show no face, head, silhouette, or exposed body; at most, the extreme lower edge may contain one indistinct dark-wrapped forearm matching his reference. Fomórach lies on his back in torn late-eighteenth-century frontier clothing, staring upward in shock and reaching weakly toward the viewer. Two small, clean fang marks are visible at the side of his neck with no blood, open wound, or gore. From those marks, branching black veins spread beneath his pallid skin like ink corruption or frost cracks, visibly beginning the Wendigo curse and the transition from his recognizable human identity toward his later Nosferatu form. Snow falls through moonlight around him and gathers on his hair, shoulders, broken timbers, and dead leaves; his breath has stopped while the surrounding forest remains violently alive. Keep the transformation localized and readable rather than grotesque, and reserve a broad calm, dark, low-detail area for overlay text.",
